{"cases":[{"target_alias":"SRC_001","attack":true,"case":"The 'Corrective Friction' criterion conflates a causal mechanism for *demonstrating* a reliability gain with the mechanism that *produces* it. A checklist can genuinely improve reliability by enforcing systematic coverage of verification steps—eliminating omissions and fatigue-driven errors—without ever triggering a documented 'Initial Guess -> Conflict -> Revision' trajectory. If the panel's members individually form no initial intuitive lean (because the checklist is applied prospectively as a procedure rather than retrospectively as a check), the target's own countercondition admits the friction signal becomes invisible, yet the reliability gain can still be real. The target thus fails to identify evidence that distinguishes genuine reliability gain from compliance in the most common case: procedural checklist application where there is no pre-checklist intuition to conflict with. The causal mechanism it demands (conflict-driven correction) is only one sub-type of how checklists reduce error, and it provides no distinguishing evidence for the complementary, arguably more common case of error-elimination through prospective proceduralization.","counterexample":[{"scenario":"Panel members apply the checklist prospectively as a step-by-step procedure on an objective verification task (e.g., building-inspection defect identification). Each member works through items systematically without first forming a holistic intuitive judgment. The panel outperforms a single expert. There are zero 'Initial Guess -> Conflict -> Revision' trajectories because there are no initial guesses. By the target's criterion, this would be classified as 'mere compliance' despite the reliability gain being genuine, systematic, and causally driven by the checklist's error-elimination function."]},{"target_alias":"SRC_002","attack":true,"case":"The target's causal mechanism ('shifting expertise from executor to instrument' via variance reduction) fails to identify any evidence within a single evaluation that would distinguish a genuine reliability gain from mere checklist compliance that happens to track the right answer. The mechanism explains why a checklist *could* reduce variance, but it never specifies what observable evidence would separate a panel that is genuinely more reliable from one that is merely mechanically compliant on the specific evaluated tasks. A panel could produce low-variance, algorithmically identical outputs that are correct on the evaluated set purely because the checklist's fixed steps happen to align with those tasks' requirements, without the checklist possessing any generalizable reliability property. The target demands a causal mechanism but provides only a restatement of the checklist's *intended* function (enforcing cognitive coverage), not an identification of the evidence that would confirm that function is the actual causal driver of correctness rather than coincidental task-checklist alignment.","counterexample":[{"scenario":"A checklist for evaluating mortgage applications lists 20 linear verification items. The non-expert panel applies all 20 items with perfect compliance and achieves 95% accuracy on the evaluated loan batch. However, only 5 of the 20 items are actually predictive of default in this batch; the other 15 are irrelevant but harmless. The panel's reliability gain over the expert (who achieves 85%) is real on this evaluation, but it is driven by the 5 relevant items happening to be included, not by the checklist's 'systematic coverage' per se. Had the batch contained loans where a non-linear interaction (excluded by the countercondition) was decisive, the same checklist would have failed. The target provides no within-evaluation evidence criterion to distinguish this coincidental alignment from a genuine, mechanism-driven reliability gain."}]}]}